@@@@ open-tcp-handshake
situation: open
topic: The TCP three-way handshake
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"must":["round trip"],"askMustNot":["7001","SYN-ACK","SYN and ACK","SYN\\+ACK"]}
noverify: an opening; it asks for the reply's flags and acknowledgement number and states neither
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Every TCP connection starts with the three-way handshake: an HTTP request, a database session, a gRPC call between services, a peer link between blockchain nodes. It costs one round trip before any data moves, which is the reason connection pools and keep-alive exist. "Connection refused", "connection timed out" and a SYN flood are all handshake outcomes, so knowing the exchange lets you tell them apart in a packet capture. It also sets the starting sequence numbers that TCP's reliability is built on afterwards.

<ask type="predict">A client opens a connection by sending a SYN with sequence number 7000. Which flags are set on the server's reply, and what acknowledgement number does it carry?</ask>

