@@@@ open-refresh-subnet-mask
situation: open
topic: Subnet masks and host counts
options: {"refresh":true}
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"must":["192\\.168\\.14\\.77/27"],"mustNot":["192\\.168\\.14\\.64","192\\.168\\.14\\.95","\\b30 (usable|hosts)"]}
@@ reply
**Problem 1**

A host has the address 192.168.14.77/27. Find the network address of its subnet, the broadcast address, and the number of usable host addresses. Apply the usual rule that the first and last addresses of a subnet are not given to hosts.

<solution>A /27 leaves 5 host bits, so blocks of 2^5 = 32 addresses in the last octet: 0, 32, 64, 96. 77 falls in the block starting at 64. Network 192.168.14.64, broadcast 192.168.14.95 (the last address before 96), usable hosts 32 − 2 = 30.</solution>
<check lang="python">
import ipaddress
n = ipaddress.ip_interface('192.168.14.77/27').network
assert str(n.network_address) == '192.168.14.64'
assert str(n.broadcast_address) == '192.168.14.95'
assert n.num_addresses - 2 == 30
</check>

<ask type="problem">The network address, the broadcast address and the host count, with your working.</ask>
@@ verify
import ipaddress
n = ipaddress.ip_interface('192.168.14.77/27').network
s = solution(reply)
assert str(n.network_address) == '192.168.14.64' and '192.168.14.64' in s
assert str(n.broadcast_address) == '192.168.14.95' and '192.168.14.95' in s
assert n.num_addresses == 32 == 2**5 and n.num_addresses - 2 == 30 and '32 − 2 = 30' in s
assert 64 <= 77 < 96
# the answer is not in what the learner reads
v = visible(reply)
assert '14.64' not in v and '14.95' not in v

@@@@ open-prior-arp-off-subnet
situation: open-prior
topic: ARP and MAC addresses
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"ARP and MAC addresses","daysAgo":4,"finished":false,"plan":["What a MAC address identifies","The ARP request and reply","Sending off the subnet through the gateway"],"right":3,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["Host A sends a packet to an address outside its own subnet. Whose MAC address does the frame carry as its destination?"],"open":"Why does an ARP cache entry expire instead of being kept for ever?"}]}
expect: {"must":["gratuitous ARP|failover"],"askMustNot":["gateway","router","192\\.168\\.1\\.1\\b"]}
noverify: an opening; the question's answer (ARP for the default gateway, frame addressed to the gateway's MAC) is not stated
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

ARP turns an IP address into the MAC address a frame needs, so every packet a host puts on Ethernet or Wi-Fi depends on it. It explains faults you will meet in practice: a duplicate address, a stale cache entry, and failover designs where a virtual IP moves between machines by gratuitous ARP. You have the request and reply exchange from last time; the case of a destination off the subnet is the one that did not hold, and it decides how every packet bound for the internet is framed.

<ask type="short">A laptop at 192.168.1.20/24 sends a packet to 8.8.8.8. For which IP address does it send its ARP request, and which machine's MAC address becomes the frame's destination?</ask>

@@@@ open-prior-congestion-from-flow-control
situation: open-prior
topic: TCP congestion control
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"TCP flow control and the sliding window","daysAgo":2,"finished":true,"plan":["The advertised window","Bytes in flight","Zero windows and window probes"],"right":4,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["The receiver advertises 8000 bytes and 6000 bytes are unacknowledged. How many more bytes may the sender send?"]}]}
expect: {"must":["flow control","in flight"],"askMustNot":["\\b1500\\b","\\b3000\\b"]}
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Congestion control is how a TCP sender sets its rate when the limit is the network rather than the receiver. It is why a download ramps up, why one lost packet can halve throughput, and why algorithms such as CUBIC and BBR are chosen with care on busy servers and CDNs. It rests on the idea from your flow control lesson: the sender may only have a window's worth of unacknowledged bytes in flight. Congestion control adds a second window that the sender works out for itself, and the smaller of the two applies. The in-flight calculation was the one miss last time, so that comes first.

<ask type="short">A sender has transmitted every byte below sequence number 15000 and has been acknowledged up to 12000. The receiver advertises a window of 4500 bytes. How many more bytes may the sender transmit right now?</ask>
@@ verify
# the answer the ask withholds: window minus bytes in flight
in_flight = 15000 - 12000
assert in_flight == 3000 and 4500 - in_flight == 1500
import re
assert not re.search(r'\b1500\b', reply) and not re.search(r'\b3000\b', reply)
# different numbers from the stored question (8000 - 6000 = 2000)
assert 8000 - 6000 == 2000 and '8000' not in reply

